1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:08,511
Modulation is the answer to an embarrassing question:
a perfect radio wave says absolutely nothing.

2
00:00:08,431 --> 00:00:16,835
A pure, steady carrier is just a hum in the spectrum — endlessly
repeating, perfectly predictable, carrying zero information.

3
00:00:16,755 --> 00:00:19,737
To make a wave say something, you have to
change it.

4
00:00:19,657 --> 00:00:27,712
Modulation is the art of changing a carrier wave in step with a message
— and every radio scheme in history is a variation on that one move.

5
00:00:27,862 --> 00:00:38,275
Remember the three properties from the last chapter's write-up — a sine wave has an
amplitude, a frequency, and a phase, and those are the only things a transmitter can touch.

6
00:00:38,195 --> 00:00:41,072
So there are only three families of
modulation.

7
00:00:40,992 --> 00:00:46,354
Everything from nineteen-twenties broadcasting
to your phone is built from these three knobs.

8
00:00:46,504 --> 00:00:54,711
Turn the amplitude knob and you get AM: the message is written into
the carrier's height — its envelope literally traces the audio.

9
00:00:54,631 --> 00:01:01,270
Simple, but noise also lives in amplitude,
which is why AM crackles in a thunderstorm.

10
00:01:01,190 --> 00:01:11,973
Turn the frequency knob and you get FM: the message speeds up and slows down the carrier's
wiggle while the height stays constant — so amplitude noise mostly wipes right off.

11
00:01:11,893 --> 00:01:24,082
That constant envelope is why FM sounds cleaner than AM, and the third knob, phase, nudges
the carrier's timing — closely related to FM, and the foundation of almost everything digital.

12
00:01:24,232 --> 00:01:27,667
Analog modulation varies the carrier
continuously.

13
00:01:27,587 --> 00:01:38,022
Digital modulation makes one crucial change: it switches the carrier among
a finite menu of states, called symbols, each standing for one or more bits.

14
00:01:37,942 --> 00:01:43,049
Shift between discrete frequencies and
it's FSK — frequency-shift keying.

15
00:01:42,969 --> 00:01:45,219
Discrete phases: PSK.

16
00:01:45,139 --> 00:01:48,539
Combine amplitude and phase levels: QAM.

17
00:01:48,459 --> 00:02:00,357
Draw the menu as dots on a plane — amplitude out from the center, phase around it — and you get
the constellation diagram, the map every digital receiver uses to decide which symbol just arrived.

18
00:02:00,507 --> 00:02:03,687
And here is the trade that shapes all of
digital radio.

19
00:02:03,607 --> 00:02:08,945
More dots on the map means more bits per
symbol — faster data in the same bandwidth.

20
00:02:08,865 --> 00:02:16,457
But more dots also means the dots sit closer together,
so it takes less noise to smudge one into its neighbor.

21
00:02:16,377 --> 00:02:19,556
Fast-but-fragile, or slow-but-tough.

22
00:02:19,476 --> 00:02:27,009
Wi-Fi at close range runs hundreds of points; a police
radio fighting fades across a whole city runs just four.

23
00:02:27,159 --> 00:02:30,721
That's exactly what GopherTrunk spends its
life demodulating.

24
00:02:30,641 --> 00:02:49,122
The land-mobile systems it decodes chose rugged, low-order schemes on purpose: four-level FSK — four frequency steps, two bits
per symbol — for DMR and NXDN, and a rotating four-phase scheme called pi-over-four DQPSK for TETRA and P25's simulcast flavor.

25
00:02:49,042 --> 00:02:58,143
Once the signal is digitized, demodulation is just measuring amplitude,
frequency, and phase — the same three knobs — and mapping them back to bits.

26
00:02:59,093 --> 00:03:10,399
So: modulation turns a mute carrier into a messenger, there are exactly three knobs to
turn, and digital radio is a menu of symbol states traded off between speed and toughness.

27
00:03:10,319 --> 00:03:12,291
